# Discord Developer Portal -> Bot -> Token. Never commit the real value.
DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=
# Discord Developer Portal -> General Information -> Application ID.
DISCORD_APP_ID=

# Postgres connection string used by the bot.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://merlin:changeme@postgres:5432/merlin?sslmode=disable
# Credentials for the postgres container itself (docker-compose.yml).
POSTGRES_USER=merlin
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme

# Path to the bootstrap config file (see config.example.yaml, just log
# level now; guild settings are DB-backed, see below). Must be an absolute
# path matching the container-side mount target in
# docker-compose.yml/docker-compose.prod.yml (the distroless base image's
# working directory isn't "/", so a relative path is fragile).
MERLIN_CONFIG_PATH=/config.yaml

# A Discord user ID that always has admin access to /config, in every guild,
# regardless of what's configured in internal/settings (DB-backed). This is
# the one identity that guarantees you can always recover if a guild's admin
# list is ever wiped or misconfigured. Set it to your own Discord user ID.
# Never DB-backed, never delegatable.
MERLIN_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USER_ID=

# debug | info | warn | error. Overrides log_level in config.yaml when set,
# so verbosity can be raised during an incident by editing .env and
# restarting, without touching the mounted config file.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Emergency stop. When set to 1/true/yes/on, every destructive Discord action
# (channel rotation, archive deletion, jail, role changes) is refused
# process-wide, in every guild, while read and inspect commands keep working.
# This is the host-level lever for when the bot is actively doing damage and
# you can't reach Discord or the database. The per-guild equivalents are
# /config pause and /config dryrun. Takes effect on restart.
MERLIN_PAUSE_ALL_WRITES=

# merlin requests Discord's privileged GUILD_MEMBERS gateway intent by
# default, so a jailed member who leaves and rejoins is re-jailed the instant
# they return rather than on the next one-minute sweep.
#
# This requires "Server Members Intent" to be ticked for the application under
# Bot in the Discord Developer Portal. If it isn't, Discord refuses the
# connection and the bot exits at startup saying so; it does not run on in a
# degraded state. Set this to 1 only if you cannot enable the portal toggle;
# jail still survives a rejoin without the intent, the sweep just takes up to
# a minute to notice.
MERLIN_DISABLE_GUILD_MEMBERS_INTENT=

# merlin can DM a server's owner a one-time pointer to /config setup the
# first time she joins a server nobody has configured yet. Off by default.
#
# This is the opposite default from the intent above, on purpose. That one is
# opt-out because ticking "Server Members Intent" in the Developer Portal is
# a visible action that should mean what it looks like it means. This is a
# message merlin sends unprompted to somebody who has not interacted with
# her, so "only if asked for" is the conservative direction rather than the
# surprising one. Nothing breaks while it is off: an owner who never gets the
# DM runs /config setup, which is exactly what the DM would have told them.
#
# Note before enabling it on a bot that is already in several servers: the
# "already nudged" timestamp is only written after a successful send, so
# turning this on DMs the owner of every still-unconfigured guild on the next
# restart, all at once. Takes effect on restart.
MERLIN_ENABLE_ONBOARDING_DM=

# internal/plugins/aimod reads messages to check them against Discord's own
# Community Guidelines. That needs the privileged MESSAGE_CONTENT gateway
# intent, which is off unless this is set.
#
# The opposite default from the members intent above, deliberately. That one
# is opt-out because ticking "Server Members Intent" in the Developer Portal
# should mean what it looks like it means. This one is a far larger ask: it
# is every message in every server the bot is in, Discord reviews it above
# 100 guilds, and spec.MD's least-privilege section names it specifically as
# the intent to request only where a plugin genuinely needs it. A deployment
# not running AI moderation has no business asking to read its members'
# conversations.
#
# Requires "Message Content Intent" ticked under Bot in the Developer Portal
# as well. If it isn't, Discord refuses the connection and the bot exits at
# startup saying so. Without this the aimod plugin still registers its
# commands and scans nothing, and /aimod status explains why. Takes effect on
# restart.
MERLIN_ENABLE_MESSAGE_CONTENT_INTENT=

# A base64-encoded 32-byte key used to encrypt per-guild third-party API keys
# at rest. Today that means the two model gateway keys each server's admin can
# set with /aimod configure key, an OrcaRouter one and an OpenRouter one. Both
# are live billing credentials: anyone who reads one can spend that person's
# money.
#
# Generate one with:
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Without it, /aimod configure key refuses rather than storing a payment
# credential in the clear, and everything else keeps working (the free
# pattern checks still run). Rotating it makes already-stored keys
# undecryptable: /aimod status says so per guild and each admin re-enters
# theirs. Nothing else in the database is encrypted with it.
#
# Treat it like DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN. It is not in config.yaml on purpose.
MERLIN_SECRET_KEY=

# The chains aimod's tip jar can read (/aimod funding).
#
# Which chains a guild's jar is read on is never configured here, and never
# stored: it is derived from the donation address itself. A 0x address is the
# same account on every EVM chain, since one key controls all of them, so it
# is read on Base, Ethereum, Polygon, Arbitrum and BNB Chain and the balances
# are summed. A T address is TRON, and a 32 byte base58 one is Solana. That is
# deliberate: the networks named under an address on screen are what somebody
# is about to send money on, and a stored network that disagreed with the
# address above it would be this bot causing the loss.
#
# All empty means the compiled-in defaults, which is the normal case. Every
# token contract there was verified live against its own symbol() and
# decimals() before being added, which is not ceremony: BNB Chain's USDT and
# USDC are 18 decimals rather than the usual 6, Base's USDbC reports a symbol
# one character off native USDC, and Polygon's bridged USDC.e reports the
# symbol "USDC" identically to the native token.
#
# To override, set an endpoint per chain and a token contract per rail:
#
#   MERLIN_RPC_<CHAIN>            e.g. MERLIN_RPC_BASE, MERLIN_RPC_SOLANA
#   MERLIN_TOKEN_<CHAIN>_<ASSET>  e.g. MERLIN_TOKEN_BASE_USDC
#
# Chains: BASE, ETHEREUM, POLYGON, ARBITRUM, BSC, TRON, SOLANA.
# Assets: USDC, USDT (not every chain carries both; see fundingRails).
#
# Set an endpoint and its token contract together, or neither. That pair is
# what a rail is, and an endpoint on one chain with a token address from
# another reports a zero balance rather than an error, which is the worst way
# for a mistake here to present.
#
# The public endpoints are fine at one read per funded guild every 15 minutes;
# point these at your own RPC if you run many servers. TronGrid throttles
# harder without an API key, which at this rate does not bite.
#
# The four names below are the superseded single-chain form, still honored so
# a deployed .env keeps working: they map onto base and tron respectively. A
# MERLIN_RPC_* value for the same chain wins over them.
MERLIN_ETH_RPC_URL=
MERLIN_USDC_CONTRACT=
MERLIN_TRON_RPC_URL=
MERLIN_USDT_CONTRACT=
